# Persistent memory (PMDK)

Intel's Persistent Memory Development Kit: the closest prior art to the store in
`research/persistence.dj`, and dead. Referenced from there.

It built a transactional object store in persistent memory — a mapped file you
allocate from and pointer-chase, the shape `research/persistence.dj` chooses — on
the SNIA NVM Programming Model. <https://pmem.io/pmdk/>

*What it did.*

- *A pool is a heap.* `pmemobj_create` makes a pool (a file, or a pool set across
  devices, `posix_fallocate`d to size) and `pmemobj_open` maps it; you allocate
  from it with `pmemobj_alloc`, never `malloc`. The library's own overhead is
  "approximately 4-8 kilobytes for each memory pool in use", and allocation is
  aligned to a cache line.
- *Pointers are offsets.* A `PMEMoid` is a persistent pointer — a pool id and an
  offset, not an address — because "persistent memory regions are mapped into
  different addresses on each system instantiation". A typed `TOID` carries a
  compile-time type as well. This is the "references are offsets" constraint.
- *A root object is how open finds the structure.* `pmemobj_root` hands back the
  root; nothing is found by scanning.
- *Open is gated on a layout name.* `pmemobj_create`/`pmemobj_open` take a
  `layout` string, "not interpreted by libpmemobj, but may be used as a check":
  open fails if it does not match the pool. That is the shape of the
  abort-on-an-unknown-tag in `research/persistence.dj`, at coarse grain.
- *Transactions.* `TX_BEGIN`/`TX_END`; `pmemobj_tx_add_range` snapshots old bytes
  for rollback, and `pmemobj_tx_alloc` "postpones persistent memory modifications
  to the commit phase", so a torn or aborted transaction is "entirely completed
  or discarded".
- *Publish.* `pmemobj_publish` / `pmemobj_action` is the delayed single-store
  publication of a prepared change — the store `research/persistence.dj` asks
  for. The mechanism, from its own example, is reserve, populate and persist
  without touching persistent state, then make a set of actions visible together:
  "The publication is fail-safe atomic in the scope of the entire collection of
  actions."
- *Defrag is compaction.* `pmemobj_defrag` relocates objects and atomically
  updates the pointers given to it, and the manual requires *all* pointers to an
  object be supplied, "so, even in the presence of failures, all pointers to the
  object will either point to the old or a new location". That is the mid-life
  move hazard `research/persistence.dj` names, and the edge pass is its
  mechanism.
- *A consistency check at rest.* `pmemobj_check` is a read-only check; a pool it
  finds inconsistent "will result in undefined behavior" if used. Startup
  validation, with the library's own definition of consistent.

*What does not transfer.*

- *The dependency is gone.* Intel wound down Optane in 2022 and PMDK feature
  development with it; `github.com/pmem/pmdk` is archived to `daos-stack/pmdk`,
  and `libpmemobj-cpp` states "no further support or maintenance planned". A
  design to copy, not a library to link.
- *It wants hardware this machine lacks.* DAX — load/store without paging — is
  the point, and it is the pmem tier `research/persistence.dj` rules out.
- *Its flush discipline is its own.* On pmem a store is not durable until flushed
  and fenced, and libpmemobj does that, choosing `pmem_persist` or `msync` by
  whether the pool is pmem or a regular file. Here the pool is a regular file
  behind the page cache, where the kernel orders and writes back, so no explicit
  flush is needed — the premise at the top of `research/persistence.dj`. Same code
  shape, opposite flush story, because the tier differs.
- *Bad blocks are a surface.* Opening a pool with bad blocks can be aborted by
  `SIGBUS` unless `CHECK_BAD_BLOCKS` is set. That is exactly the "corruption is
  local" problem the kernel does not solve; our answer is per-unit checksums and
  skip, not a signal.

*What to take:* the pool-as-heap, offset `OID`s with typed pointers, the root
object, the layout gate at open, transactional undo with deferred publish, and
defrag-at-the-edges — which is the compaction in `research/persistence.dj`, and
the reason it must not run while readers hold pointers.